<!-- ── Debrief (shown after completion) ── -->
<div class="lab-debrief" id="lab-debrief">
  <div class="lab-debrief-label">Debrief — Server-Side Request Forgery</div>

  <h3>What happened</h3>
  <p>CloudSnap's URL import feature accepts any URL and fetches it server-side using <code>fetch()</code> without validation. Because the request originates from the server (not the user's browser), it can reach internal services that are not exposed to the internet — including cloud metadata endpoints that serve IAM credentials.</p>

  <h3>The vulnerable code</h3>
  <pre>const response = await fetch(userUrl);
// userUrl comes directly from the request body — no validation
// Server fetches ANY URL, including localhost and internal IPs</pre>

  <h3>The fix</h3>
  <p>Validate URLs against an allowlist of permitted hosts. Resolve hostnames to IP addresses and block private/internal ranges (127.0.0.0/8, 10.0.0.0/8, 169.254.0.0/16, etc.). Disable HTTP redirects to prevent bypass. On AWS, enable IMDSv2 which requires a PUT request with a token header.</p>
  <pre>// 1. Allowlist permitted hosts
if (!allowedHosts.includes(parsed.hostname)) return 403;

// 2. Resolve and block private IPs
const ip = await dns.resolve(parsed.hostname);
if (isPrivateIp(ip)) return 403;

// 3. Disable redirects
fetch(url, { redirect: 'error' });

// 4. Enable IMDSv2 on AWS (requires token)
aws ec2 modify-instance-metadata-options \
  --http-tokens required</pre>

  <h3>Real-world impact</h3>
  <p>In a real SSRF attack, the stolen IAM credentials grant the attacker direct access to cloud resources — S3 buckets, databases, secrets managers — without needing to compromise the application further. The credentials are typically short-lived but can be used to pivot deeper into the infrastructure.</p>
  <p><strong>Capital One (2019):</strong> An attacker exploited an SSRF vulnerability in a misconfigured web application firewall to access the AWS metadata service. The stolen IAM credentials provided access to S3 buckets containing 100 million credit card applications and 140,000 Social Security numbers. The attacker was caught after bragging about it on social media.</p>

  <h3>How to find this in the wild</h3>
  <p>Look for any feature that fetches external resources — URL imports, webhook configurations, PDF generators, URL previews. Test by pointing the URL to a server you control (like Burp Collaborator or interactsh) to confirm the server makes outbound requests. Then try internal addresses: <code>127.0.0.1</code>, <code>169.254.169.254</code>, and internal hostnames.</p>

